1
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:16,620
CONVERSATION 1
OK then, this here is your yearly performance review.

2
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:22,980
You've been with the company for 3 years and under my direct supervision for the past 12 months.

3
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,260
Your report is mostly good.

4
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,420
But there are a couple of issues we must discuss.

5
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,940
The report is yours to take with you.

6
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:33,600
We'll go through the main points together now.

7
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,560
And you can come through it in greater detail later.

8
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,060
Do you have any questions before we start?

9
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:41,200
No, sir.

10
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:42,580
I'll get straight to it then.

11
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:47,200
It is evident that you are a hard worker.

12
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,200
You are punctual and industrious.

13
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:54,720
Furthermore, you have strong analytical skills and attention to detail.

14
00:02:54,720 --> 00:03:00,760
The company, myself included, are happy with your productivity and overall performance.

15
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:01,520
Thank you.

16
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:03,760
I appreciate the recognition.

17
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:06,960
Do you agree, then, with this depiction so far?

18
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:10,980
Yeah, it's a little simplistic perhaps.

19
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:14,500
But that's to be expected from this kind of evaluation.

20
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:15,640
Sure.

21
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,940
Would you want to add anything?

22
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:22,260
Your comments can be added to the report if you wish.

23
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:23,520
Oh, there's no need.

24
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:25,680
It's a flattering portrayal really.

25
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:27,220
Thank you, though.

26
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:28,760
OK.

27
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:32,720
This leads me to the not-so-good part.

28
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:39,840
This mostly centers around one behavioral aspect—your ability to work with others.

29
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:41,000
I see.

30
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,180
Yes.

31
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:50,280
As you already know, over the last several months, there have been some complaints raised about your communication style.

32
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:54,140
Of course, we already had like three meetings about this.

33
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:59,560
Look, all I'm going to say is that it was never my intention to offend anyone.

34
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:06,220
Simply put, I don't like to write long emails, because, frankly, I think they're a waste of time.

35
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:08,620
Conciseness is efficiency.

36
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:15,180
I feel that writing long emails hurts that same productivity you praised me for just a minute ago.

37
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:18,620
And I already apologized if anyone was offended.

38
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:32,360
But to be perfectly honest, I think anyone who was offended was being a little too sensitive.

39
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:50,040
Q1. What are the speakers most probably doing?

40
00:04:50,040 --> 00:05:07,560
Q2. What does the woman say she appreciates?

41
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:26,260
Q3. What has given rise to some complaints about the woman?

42
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:45,580
Q4. Why doesn't the woman like to write long emails?

43
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:50,080
CONVERSATION 2
So tell me, why do you wish to study medicine?

44
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:52,120
I've always wanted to be a doctor.

45
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:55,820
I come from a small town where healthcare was never the best.

46
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:58,820
And I think that had a profound effect on me.

47
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:05,020
As a child, I remember thinking how great it would be to serve the community and cure people!

48
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:10,120
What other people or events in your life have shaped your interest in becoming a doctor?

49
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:12,260
Well, that's hard to say.

50
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:15,660
I would certainly say my parents have been a key factor.

51
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,960
They always pushed me to study hard.

52
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:21,360
And I certainly wouldn't be here without their support.

53
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:26,500
I guess I was also immensely fortunate to have good teachers and friends around me.

54
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:34,600
At the end of the day, I've always been surrounded by a positive environment conducive to my working hard and pursuing my dreams.

55
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:37,920
Is there any medical branch that you would like to specialize in?

56
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:40,180
I haven't decided yet.

57
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:42,200
It's still early days.

58
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:46,300
However, if I had to say, internal medicine.

59
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:48,400
Why internal medicine?

60
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:49,960
I know it's no picnic.

61
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:56,980
But I find it the most intriguing, because it covers so much and ties together so many other specialties.

62
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:57,820
Thank you.

63
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:01,080
Now I'd like to address your academic history and projected grades.

64
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:10,860
We like to think of ourselves as a prestigious institution with a highly competitive and promising student body.

65
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:17,160
As you know, to get into our programmes, you need at least 85% score in the entrance exam.

66
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:19,080
Do you think you can attain this?

67
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:20,160
I think so.

68
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:21,240
Yes.

69
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:25,160
I'm looking at your school grades now and I'll be honest.

70
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:27,060
It's a bit of a mixed bag.

71
00:07:27,060 --> 00:07:31,640
You do get top grades here and there, but you are not very...

72
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:34,180
Shall we say "consistent"?

73
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:53,297
I see no problem in making you a conditional offer, but consider yourself warned, our entrance exam is, to use your own words, no picnic.

74
00:07:53,297 --> 00:08:10,120
Q5. What does the woman say she has always wanted to do since childhood?

75
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:28,320
Q6. What do we learn about the woman's parents from the conversation?

76
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:47,220
Q7. Why does the woman say she wants to choose internal medicine as her specialty?

77
00:08:47,220 --> 00:09:31,980
Q8. How does the man describe the woman's school grades?

78
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:36,960
PASSAGE 1
Logging into your email account can sometimes feel like trying to hack into the Pentagon.

79
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:56,820
If you follow all of the guidelines, you need to use a different password for every one of your accounts; use an incomprehensible variety of letters, numbers, and symbols; sign up for two-step verification; and change every password every time one of them is compromised.

80
00:09:56,820 --> 00:10:09,940
More often than not, you then have to choose photos that include a street sign, as this apparently provides solid evidence to your computer that you are, in fact, a human being.

81
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:16,160
It's no small wonder most of us forget our passwords more often than our keys.

82
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:30,720
Now a new peer-reviewed study by researchers at Aalto University in Finland is attempting to figure out why we forget our passwords in order to help put an end to this frustrating phenomenon.

83
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:42,360
Their findings suggest that how likely you are to remember your password has less to do with the intricacy of the password itself and more to do with how often you anticipate using it.

84
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:59,500
Which is to say: you are far more likely to remember a complicated password if you know you'll frequently be using it to log into an account than you are to remember a simple password for an account that you don't expect to log into very often.

85
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:17,060
The researchers advise websites to include more of an incentive for users to log in more often in order to help them remember their secret codes.

86
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:38,220
Q9. Why do we often have to choose photos that include a street sign to log into our email accounts?

87
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:58,360
Q10. What do researchers at a university in Finland aim to do in their new study?

88
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:18,420
Q11. What do the researchers advise websites to do at the end of the passage?

89
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:22,900
PASSAGE 2
Four years ago, Jenny Weiner was in a fitness slump.

90
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:26,620
She wanted to improve her physical and mental health.

91
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:31,520
However, her workouts lacked intensity without a proper training programme to follow.

92
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:44,120
When she went to the gym, her daily ritual predominantly involved easy exercise options like jogging; she avoided more formidable exercises like lifting weights.

93
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:48,400
A personal trainer who might push her was out of the question.

94
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:52,020
The extravagant cost was just unaffordable.

95
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:55,600
She then discovered the fitness phone app Slimmeretics.

96
00:12:55,600 --> 00:13:05,400
To use the app, you first input your profile about your previous training experiences and preferences, as well as your desired goals.

97
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:12,720
A virtual coach then uses that information to suggest a personalised training regime.

98
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:27,520
After the workout, the app's artificial intelligence software system uses feedback from Ms. Weiner and its other 53 million users around the world to adjust future training sessions.

99
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:30,960
Now Ms. Weiner finds the workouts exciting.

100
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:34,160
This is because no two are ever the same.

101
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:38,800
As a result, the app has helped her to lose more than 25 kilograms.

102
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:45,600
The app's mathematical approach can arrange a more meticulous workout schedule.

103
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:48,840
This has led to optimal results.

104
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:54,780
However, some experts think that apps cannot replace human personal trainers.

105
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:58,100
An app cannot understand human behavior.

106
00:13:58,100 --> 00:14:03,920
It will not comprehend that there are competing interests outside of health and fitness.

107
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:08,540
Additionally, a human trainer can add compassion to the workout.

108
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:13,400
He can also make exercise fun and sustainable.

109
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:18,340
Trainers can monitor whether a person is doing exercises correctly.

110
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:21,240
This can safeguard against injuries.

111
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:28,627
An app cannot perceive such subtleties.

112
00:14:28,627 --> 00:14:52,020
Q12. What was the problem Jenny Weiner encountered four years ago?

113
00:14:52,020 --> 00:15:11,880
Q13. What can the fitness phone app Slimmeretics do according to the passage?

114
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:32,000
Q14. Why does the passage say Jenny Weiner now finds the workouts exciting?

115
00:15:32,000 --> 00:16:18,540
Q15. What do some experts think of fitness apps like Slimmeretics?

