00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:28,820
RECORDING 1
All societies are held together by seven universal moral rules, which include deferring to superiors and respecting the property of others, Oxford University has concluded.

117
00:16:28,820 --> 00:16:46,520
Although many cultures are moving towards more liberal, less hierarchical organizations, the new research suggests that traditional power structures and basic values of charity and brotherhood are the cornerstones of successful societies.

118
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:54,560
The huge study of 60 different cultures around the world found that all communities operate under seven basic moral codes.

119
00:16:54,560 --> 00:17:08,940
Those universal rules are: help your family, help your group, return favours, be brave, defer to superiors, divide resources fairly and respect the property of others.

120
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:25,440
The character traits held for every kind of community, be they traditional hunter-gatherers or advanced civilizations, helping to uphold civilized society and foster social cooperation, researchers found.

121
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:35,400
"Everyone everywhere shares a common moral code," said Dr. Oliver Scott Curry, lead author and senior researcher at Oxford.

122
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:45,240
"These seven moral rules appear to be universal across cultures because people face the same social problems.

123
00:17:45,240 --> 00:18:03,700
Even if some of these traits look right-wing or conservative, for example, deferring to authority, left-wing people will still have a group loyalty and deference to someone or something."

124
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:20,080
The study, published in Current Anthropology, is the largest and most comprehensive cross-cultural survey of morality ever conducted.

125
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:29,360
The team examined the ethnographic records of 60 societies from around the world.

126
00:18:29,360 --> 00:19:59,900
They found that the seven rules were considered morally good in all societies and were observed across all continents, religions, and politics.

127
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:08,221
Q16. What did the researchers at Oxford University conclude?

128
00:20:08,221 --> 00:20:18,500
Q17. What was the purpose of the huge study of 60 different cultures?

129
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:39,580
Q18. What does Dr. Oliver Scott Curry suggest researchers do?

130
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:43,300
RECORDING 2
When it comes to food, color is money.

131
00:20:43,300 --> 00:20:53,060
Food companies know that better color gives better prices, so they ensure that their products are attractive in color to bolster earnings.

132
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:58,680
Oranges, for example, have to meet specific color standards, and oranges are just the beginning.

133
00:20:58,680 --> 00:21:09,160
There are color standards for foods including French fries, tomatoes, pumpkins, olives, honey, and cherries.

134
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:12,180
The attention to color is for good reason.

135
00:21:12,180 --> 00:21:19,340
For all the talk of the tongue, our eyes really might be the most important part of our eating experience.

136
00:21:19,340 --> 00:21:34,440
A great number of our brain functions are dedicated to processing vision, while only a percent or two is given over to taste faculties, and the result is that color actually changes how we taste food.

137
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:39,920
Scientists have demonstrated many times the importance of color to taste.

138
00:21:39,920 --> 00:21:49,580
In a 1980s study, for example, test participants were asked to name the flavor of the beverage they were drinking without being able to see that beverage.

139
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:54,440
Only one in five was able to identify it as orange.

140
00:21:54,440 --> 00:22:01,500
But when they were allowed to see what they were drinking which was orange in color, each of them identified the orange flavor.

141
00:22:01,500 --> 00:22:09,900
And when a drink that was lime flavor was colored orange, nearly half of respondents identified the flavor of the beverage as orange.

142
00:22:09,900 --> 00:22:14,020
None made that mistake when the beverage was green in color.

143
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:21,240
What's happening when someone fails to identify the flavor of a beverage they can't see or that has an abnormal color?

144
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:26,900
Is it that participants can't discern what the flavor is, and so rely on visual cues?

145
00:22:26,900 --> 00:22:31,340
Or does color actually change the experience of taste?

146
00:22:31,340 --> 00:22:36,200
The key to these questions is the effect of color on expectation.

147
00:22:36,200 --> 00:22:39,440
The brain makes predictions using color.

148
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:44,680
These predictions rely on expectations that come from experience.

149
00:22:44,680 --> 00:22:54,100
Research shows that we're all born liking sweet things and desire to eat them, but we aren't born knowing what the sweet things we crave look like.

150
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:57,320
So we need to learn this over time.

151
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:45,560
Q19. What does the speaker say about our eyes?

152
00:23:45,560 --> 00:24:04,660
Q20. What does color actually do according to this lecture?

153
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:23,320
Q21. What does the brain use color to do?

154
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:27,900
RECORDING 3
Searching for meaning is something many of us experience throughout our lives.

155
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:33,960
For some, this meaning is religious, some political and some interpersonal.

156
00:24:33,960 --> 00:24:37,900
And having a sense of meaning can bring us happiness.

157
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:46,840
A new study looks at the relationship between meaning and happiness in the context of financial resources.

158
00:24:46,840 --> 00:25:02,700
Rhia Catapano from the University of Toronto and colleagues find meaning is a far weaker predictor of happiness for rich people than poorer people, suggesting economic resources can impact how we experience meaning.

159
00:25:02,700 --> 00:25:08,800
The team analysed data from over 500,000 people across 123 countries.

160
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:19,240
The first study used data from a daily poll of US residents collected between 2013 and 2015.

161
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:34,640
Well-being was tracked using measures of positive affect—whether, during the day before they were surveyed, participants had smiled or laughed a lot or experienced a lot of happiness.

162
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:49,640
Meaning was measured through a daily "purpose index", which explored the extent to which participants felt that they like what they do every day and are motivated to achieve their goals.

163
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:53,340
Finally, income levels were assessed.

164
00:25:53,340 --> 00:26:03,440
The results showed that the correlation between meaning and happiness was strongest among those in lower income brackets.

165
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:08,880
But as income levels increased, the correlation became weaker.

166
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:13,300
The second study looked at worldwide data.

167
00:26:13,300 --> 00:26:26,980
Happiness was measured using the same questions as in the first study, while meaning was measured using the single question: "Do you feel your life has an important purpose or meaning?"

168
00:26:26,980 --> 00:26:34,140
Again, the strength of the relationship between meaning and happiness depended on participants' income.

169
00:26:34,140 --> 00:26:42,520
Meaning had a greater influence on happiness for those on lower incomes compared to those in higher brackets.

170
00:26:42,520 --> 00:26:57,600
In the final study, French participants indicated the extent to which they felt they led a purposeful and meaningful life and how much they considered themselves a happy person.

171
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:12,760
Finally, rather than using specific income brackets, they placed themselves on a social ladder, representing where they stood in terms of wealth, education and occupation.

172
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:19,180
The results from the study duplicated those of the first two.

173
00:27:19,180 --> 00:27:29,020
So overall, meaning and happiness had a stronger relationship for those with fewer resources than those with more.

174
00:27:29,020 --> 00:27:39,840
This might be the case because richer individuals have more access to other sources of happiness, many of which will be external, like lack of stress or community.

175
00:27:39,840 --> 00:27:55,108
Thus, a focus on an internal sense of satisfaction, purpose or meaning is less important.

176
00:27:55,108 --> 00:28:17,500
Q22. What does the speaker say many of us search for throughout our lives?

177
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:38,340
Q23. What do Rhia Catapano and colleagues find about economic resources?

178
00:28:38,340 --> 00:28:38,390
Q24. What do we learn about the second study conducted by the team?

179
00:28:38,390 --> 00:29:31,366
Q25. Why is meaning less important for richer people according to the speaker?
